notes='''### 040-N1 — Construction and marginal normal form

The proposed least fine extension has closed sets A∪B, where A is τ-closed in S and B is τ′_0-closed in T. It restricts to τ′_0 on T and to τ on S∖T. The margin gives a canonical description with B=X∩T, U=X∩(S∖T), U relatively closed and B containing the τ-closure of U intersected with T; then X=B∪cl_τ(U). The inverse uniqueness claim at the foot is wholly cancelled. Manuscript page 39, no date.

### 040-N2 — Formula slips and actual targeted check

The union index reads J∈I, although a distributive expansion would index subsets J⊂I. The rightmost B has an undotted J index; the index under that intersection remains j/J. The sentence after the display calls its terms closed in (S,τ), with no prime at either occurrence. That assertion is not generally true: take S={a,b,c}, T={a,b}, original closed sets ∅, T, S and the discrete refined topology on T. Then A=∅, B={a} gives a new closed set which is not τ-closed. This explicit finite case was checked directly against the listed closed sets.

The intended construction is nevertheless stable under arbitrary intersections: put A=intersection A_i and B=intersection ((A_i∩T)∪B_i), the latter taken inside T. Then intersection (A_i∪B_i)=A∪B by checking points in T and outside T separately. A is τ-closed; B is τ′_0-closed. Arbitrary unions have the same form by taking the two unions separately. Empty set and S occur, and the displayed restriction calculation is valid. This proves the proposed family is an ultratopology, and minimality follows because every sought topology must contain all A and B and their unions. None of these repairs is substituted into the diplomatic formula.

### 040-N3 — Scope

The canonical marginal bijection and the cancelled uniqueness claim were read, but not separately proved on this page. The prose before the stability argument says moins fine after cancelling plus; H’s alternative narrative obscures that point.'''
